/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (Freshwater: Critical for Indian industries including agriculture; irrigation consuming 89% of freshwater withdrawals, textiles water-intensive processing, power generation; thermal plant cooling, and beverages direct water inputs [11]. Water scarcity rapidly emerging as a structural constraint on economic growth and urban resilience. Approximately 4 billion people globally experiencing severe water scarcity for at least part of the year, reflecting persistent imbalances between freshwater demand and available supply. [12] Freshwater security issues could threaten at least USD 425 billion in business value globally, creating an economic imperative to address water scarcity [13].)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (Ocean: Coastal zones, marine ecosystems, fisheries, relevant for ports managing 80% of India's trade by volume [14], fishing communities 14.5 million people dependent on marine fisheries [15], coastal tourism, and industries relying on maritime transport directly impacting India's seafood industry and coastal livelihoods. However, overfishing, agricultural and industrial runoff, air pollution and rising temperatures threaten ocean health [16]. Around 85% of the world's marine fish stocks are either fully exploited or overfished [17]. Plastics pollution causes global externalities of $139 billion annually, with impacts on marine biodiversity [18], and by 2050, there could be more plastic in the ocean than fish by weight [19].)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (Land: Forests, agricultural land, grasslands, urban green spaces. Affected by mining, real estate, plantation agriculture, and manufacturing requiring large land footprints. India's forest cover stands at 21.71% of geographic area as of 2021, with degraded land estimated at 97.85 million hectares requiring restoration [20]. Globally, business activities such as land conversion, overgrazing, expansion of monoculture farming, and continued irrigation contribute to land degradation, with an estimated 52% of the world's agricultural land classified as moderately or severely degraded according to the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification [21]. Infrastructure expansion associated with energy production and urban development is a major driver of habitat fragmentation and biodiversity loss [21]. The Nature Risk Rising report highlighted that $44 trillion of economic value generation over half the world's total GDP is potentially at risk due to business dependence on nature and its services [22].)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (Atmosphere: Air quality and climate regulation traditionally viewed through climate policy lens, atmospheric quality affecting businesses through emission regulations, employee health, and ecosystem functions like monsoon patterns [15].)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
